#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# jacobi_symbol

#% text
# `jacobi_symbol(a, n)` gives the Jacobi symbol `(a/n)` for an odd `n > 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For a prime `n` it is the Legendre symbol: 1 when `a` is a non-zero square modulo `n`, -1 when it is not, 0 when `n` divides `a`. For composite `n` it is the product of the Legendre symbols of the prime factors.

#% item
# The value is found by quadratic reciprocity, without factoring `n`. For even or negative `n` use `kronecker_symbol`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
jacobi_symbol(2, 7)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
jacobi_symbol(3, 7)
#% output
#= -1

#% cell
jacobi_symbol(14, 7)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
jacobi_symbol(1001, 9907)
#% output
#= -1

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The quadratic residues modulo 11:

#% cell
select(range(10), a => jacobi_symbol(a, 11) == 1)
#% output
#= [1, 3, 4, 5, 9]

#% cell
union(mod(range(10)^2, 11))
#% output
#= [1, 3, 4, 5, 9]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Euler's criterion `a^((p - 1)/2) = (a/p) mod p`:

#% cell
[power_mod(5, 11, 23), mod(jacobi_symbol(5, 23), 23)]
#% output
#= [22, 22]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `kronecker_symbol`, `power_mod`, `is_prime`
